ROTATION

In rotation, we rotate the object at a particular angle θ (theta) from its origin. From the following figure, we can see that the point P(X, Y) is located at angle θ from the horizontal X coordinate with distance r from the origin.

Let us suppose we want to rotate it at the angle θ. After rotating it to a new location, we will get a new point P'(X', Y').

A' = X cosθ - Y sinθ

B' = X sinθ + Y cosθ

[A'  B'] = [A  B] [ cosθ   sinθ ]
                     [ -sinθ  cosθ ]

OR

P' = P . R

Where R is the rotation matrix, the equation can be represented in matrix form as follows –

R = [ cosθ   sinθ ]
    [ -sinθ  cosθ  ]

The above matrix can be used in the case of a rotation angle which is positive. However, for a rotation angle which is negative, the matrix is shown below –

R = [ cos(-θ)   sin(-θ) ]
    [ -sin(-θ)  cos(-θ)  ]


For negative rotation, the matrix becomes –

R = [ cosθ   -sinθ ]
    [ sinθ    cosθ  ]

(∵ cos(-θ) = cosθ and sin(-θ) = -sinθ)


